---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import SectionHeading from '../components/ui/SectionHeading.astro';
import Reveal from '../components/ui/Reveal.astro';
import Button from '../components/ui/Button.astro';
import Divider from '../components/ui/Divider.astro';
---

<PageLayout
  title="About"
  description="Personal story, career journey, and the philosophy behind Prajjwal Sharma's approach to business, marketing, and AI."
>
  <section class="px-6 pt-8 pb-20">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-content">
      <Reveal>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-accent">About</p>
        <h1 class="mt-6 max-w-2xl text-balance font-display text-4xl font-semibold text-fg sm:text-5xl">
          I started in marketing execution. I stayed for the strategy underneath it.
        </h1>
      </Reveal>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="px-6 py-16 border-t border-white/10">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-content grid gap-16 lg:grid-cols-[1fr_1.3fr]">
      <SectionHeading eyebrow="Story">Career journey</SectionHeading>
      <div class="space-y-6 text-muted">
        <p>
          My work started where most performance marketing does — running ad accounts,
          chasing click-through rates, optimizing budgets. Over six-plus years, and an MBA
          along the way, the useful part of the job turned out to be upstream of all that:
          understanding what the business actually needed before touching a single campaign.
        </p>
        <p>
          That shift is what led to freelance consulting alongside an in-house role as
          Marketing Manager, and to running a small creative and editing team for client
          work spanning SEO, social media, and digital strategy. The common thread across
          all of it is structure — breaking down a vague business problem into something
          you can actually execute and measure.
        </p>
        <p>
          More recently, that same instinct for structure has pointed toward AI — not as a
          novelty, but as leverage for the parts of strategy, research, and content work that
          used to eat the most time.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Divider />

  <section class="px-6 py-16">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-content grid gap-16 lg:grid-cols-[1fr_1.3fr]">
      <SectionHeading eyebrow="Philosophy">Business &amp; AI philosophy</SectionHeading>
      <div class="space-y-6 text-muted">
        <p>
          Good strategy is mostly good structure. Most "creative" problems in marketing and
          business are actually clarity problems — once the goal, audience, and constraints
          are properly mapped, the right approach tends to be obvious.
        </p>
        <p>
          AI is a force multiplier for that structure, not a replacement for it. It's most
          useful applied to well-defined workflows — research synthesis, content systems,
          campaign analysis — where judgment still decides the input and the output, and AI
          handles the volume in between.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Divider />

  <section class="px-6 py-16">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-content grid gap-16 lg:grid-cols-[1fr_1.3fr]">
      <SectionHeading eyebrow="Ahead">Vision &amp; goals</SectionHeading>
      <div class="space-y-6 text-muted">
        <p>
          The near-term goal is straightforward: move into a role — consulting, analyst, or
          operations — where structured thinking and applied AI are the actual job, not a
          side skill layered on top of execution work.
        </p>
        <p>
          Longer term, that points toward building — products, systems, or a practice — where
          the same combination of business judgment and AI leverage compounds on its own.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="px-6 py-24 text-center sm:py-32">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-lg">
      <h2 class="text-balance font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-fg sm:text-3xl">
        Curious how this plays out in practice?
      </h2>
      <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
        <Button href="/resume">View resume</Button>
        <Button href="/contact" variant="secondary">Get in touch</Button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</PageLayout>
